    "final_answer": "I found comprehensive information from Wikipedia's authoritative page on solar eclipses visible from the United States. Here is the answer:\n\n## Upcoming Solar Eclipses Visible in North America\n\nBased on Wikipedia's \"List of solar eclipses visible from the United States\" (last updated as of August 2026):\n\n### 🌑 The Next Visible Solar Eclipse in North America:\n**January 26, 2028** — A **partial solar eclipse** will be visible in the United States. This will be the next solar eclipse of any kind (partial, annular, or total) visible from anywhere in the U.S. / North America.\n\n### 🌑 The One After That:\n**March 30, 2033** — A **total solar eclipse**, but it will only be visible from **Alaska** (not the contiguous U.S.). This will be the next total solar eclipse visible in any part of the United States.\n\n### Additional Notable Future Eclipses:\n- **June 21, 2039** — Annular solar eclipse (Alaska only)\n- **August 22, 2044** — Total solar eclipse in the **contiguous United States** (the next one visible from the lower 48 states)\n- **February 5, 2046** — Annular solar eclipse in the contiguous U.S.\n\n### Summary:\n| Rank | Date | Type | Visibility |\n|------|------|------|------------|\n| 1st (next) | January 26, 2028 | Partial | United States |\n| 2nd | March 30, 2033 | Total | Alaska only |\n| (next in contiguous U.S.) | August 22, 2044 | Total | Contiguous U.S. |",
